Not the headline one, no. SLE is a promise to your customers — the people filing requests. Your director isn't the customer of a service request; he's the person deciding where eight people's time goes. Different audience, different artifact.

Two distinctions worth being precise about:

SLE is a target, not a measurement. The metric is your cycle-time percentile. The SLE is the promise you set from it. What's actually reportable upward is SLE attainment — "87% of Standard requests landed inside the 6-day SLE this quarter" — which is a single percentage with a trend line.

That framing will land unusually well with your audience specifically. SLE attainment is structurally identical to an SLO: a target, an attainment percentage, an error budget you're either inside or outside. A DevSecOps director already reads dashboards shaped like that. "Standard requests: 6-day SLE, 87% attainment, trending flat" is a sentence that needs no Kanban vocabulary at all.

Here's how I'd tier it:

Audience	Cadence	What they get
Director	Monthly/quarterly	Allocation vs target, SLE attainment %, outcome metrics, blocker themes needing his authority
Your manager	Fortnightly	Throughput by work item type, cycle-time percentiles, aging WIP, blocker pareto
The team	Daily	Aging WIP, WIP limit state, what's blocked

The headline for a director is allocation. That's what your manager literally asked for — direction, purpose, how the team is allocated. SLE attainment is the supporting act: it's the answer to the question he'll inevitably ask, which is some version of "but are they reliable?" Because that's the Scrum-shaped question, and without SLE attainment you have no answer to it. With it, you have a better answer than sprint completion ever gave.

The second thing a director genuinely wants is the blocker pareto, because it's the only chart on the list that contains a task for him. Everything else is reporting; that one is a request.

Two cautions.

Don't publish an SLE before the backfill. Once a director hears a number it becomes a target, and a target you invented from nothing will be wrong in a direction you don't control. Derive it from real data, then state it.

Report attainment, never individual misses. The moment the conversation becomes "which four items missed SLE," you've reconstructed sprint commitment with extra steps, and someone will start gaming it by splitting cards. The unit of accountability is the percentage and its trend.

what can insights offer here?

Architected workaround leveraging field stamping for SLE attainment visualization

The governing rule: Insights counts items and groups them by field values. It can't do math across items. So percentiles, medians, and durations are out — but anything you precompute into a field becomes chartable.

That's the whole trick for SLE attainment. Add one field:

bash
create_field "SLE Status" SINGLE_SELECT "Within SLE,Breached,No SLE"

The same Action that stamps Completed and computes Cycle Time (days) compares that number against the threshold for the item's class of service and writes the verdict. Now Insights charts it natively:

Chart	Config
Attainment trend	X-axis Iteration, Group by SLE Status, filter -class-of-service:Intangible
Attainment by type	X-axis Work Item Type, Group by SLE Status

The first one is your director chart. Stacked bars per two-week period, green-over-red, trend visible at a glance — and it's the SLO-shaped artifact I mentioned, generated in-product with no export.

So the director tier maps like this:

Artifact	Insights?
Allocation vs target	Native
SLE attainment	Native, via the stamped field
Blocker pareto	Native
Escalation demand trend	Native, historical chart
Cycle-time percentiles	No — needs generating
Aging WIP distribution	No — table view approximates it
DORA / outcome metrics	No — different system entirely

Five of seven, which is better than I'd expect from a tool this simple.

Three limits worth knowing before you promise a dashboard.

Insights only knows an item's current field values — it has no history of what a field used to be. Historical charts track open/closed state, nothing else. This is fine for your design because Iteration and SLE Status are stamped at completion and never change afterward, so filtering by iteration gives you real historical slices. It would break if you tried to chart something mutable.

There's no export or embed. You screenshot charts into a deck. Slightly annoying at director cadence, not a blocker.

And the average-cycle-time chart I suggested earlier is a trap if it becomes the number of record. Your SLE is a percentile precisely because averages hide the tail, and the tail is what stakeholders remember. Keep that chart for the team's own tuning; report attainment upward.

The percentile generator is the one thing genuinely worth building — a scheduled Action computing p50/p85/p95 from the Cycle Time (days) values, posted as a comment or committed as a chart. It's also what tells you where to set the SLE thresholds in the first place, so it comes before the SLE Status field is meaningful.
